}"#, title: "wf")
        }
        variations {
            Markdown("- **A shortcut on one element**: written inside an element's block, `on key(\"Enter\") \{ … \}` listens on that element only.\n- **A chord**: `on key(\"mod+k mod+s\") \{ … \}` is Ctrl+K then Ctrl+S, the second within 1.5 seconds.\n- **A shortcut for the whole app**: `on key(…)` at the top of `app` listens on every page.\n- **Check a combination held as data**: `keyIs(e, c.keys)` in an `on keydown(e)` handler says whether the event is that combination, so one handler can serve every command in the list.\n- **Show the shortcut beside a control**: `Tooltip(\"Save\", shortcut: \"mod+s\")` and `Menu.Item(shortcut: \"mod+s\")` write it with `keyLabel` for you.")

        }
        Markdown("Press Ctrl+K, type `sav` and press Enter: the palette closes and the page says \"Ran “Save”.\" The two theme commands call `setTheme`, which shows a change once the project names a dark theme ([Add a dark-mode switch](/recipes/dark-mode-switch)).\n\n- `on key(\"mod+k\", e) \{ … \}` at the top of the page listens on the whole document for as long as the page shows, and stops when the reader leaves it. `mod` is ⌘ on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere, which is what an app's shortcuts use. `e` is the key event: `e.preventDefault()` keeps the browser's own Ctrl+K (its search bar) and Ctrl+S (save the page) from running too.\n- The palette is a `Modal`. It traps focus while open, Escape closes it, and `on close` sets `open` back to `false` however it closed.\n- `shown` filters the commands by what is typed. Enter in the field runs the first one left; a click runs any of them.\n- `keyLabel(c.keys)` writes a shortcut the way the reader's system does: `⌘S` on a Mac, `Ctrl+S` elsewhere.\n- Every command goes through `run`, so a shortcut and the palette do the same thing, and the commands stay one list of data.")

    }
}
